# `label` only names what it is handed. Nine functions that take numbers have met at `few`, nine that take texts at `more`; one has met nobody. All nineteen are handed to `label`.
# Being named loses nobody and unites nobody: what is passed on only to be named arrives as "some function", without the name of a family. If a value that is lost already kept its name there, the two families would be united at the parameter of `label`, or not, by whether the one that has met nobody came first: one program, two answers.
